WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday. Yesterday was World Refugee Day so I'd thought I'd share a story of a family I met while I was in Jordan:

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.040
Mufak, Nada, and their five kids.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:15.680
Mufak was trained as a butcher but he'd become a successful entrepeneur in Syria, but then he had to leave everything behind in 2012 to escape the fighting

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.180
and after four years in Jordan, where refugees are not legally allowed to work, the family's savings were depleted.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.120
But when I met them back in February, they were excited because they were about to be resettled in the United States; specifically Rockford, Illinois.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:33.200
You may have heard it's easy for Syrian refugees to get approved to live in the U.S. but it's just not.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.280
There are many, many layers of background checks, involving all kinds of investigations

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.720
from iris scans to finger-printing to lots and lots and lots of interviews.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.320
In the case of this family, all told, it took more than two years but at last, they'd been approved.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.640
"We've never been to the U.S. before," Mufak told me, "we're very excited. We can't wait."

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.360
Nada told me, "I hope Americans will welcome us. I hope we do not disappoint you."

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.060
So a couple of weeks ago, Rosianna and I went to Rockford to catch up with the family.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.140
Life Magazine once said Rockford is "as nearly typical of the U.S. as any city can be".

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.460
Y'know, it's the kind of place where downtown, there's a statue of a white guy holding fireworks and an American flag.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:19.440
It was great to see the family again and we enjoyed an immense and delicious lunch together but the dislocation of the move had clearly been difficult.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.340
From the language to the weather, from the healthcare system to the grocery store to the appliances, everything was different.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:35.820
Nada explained one profound change to me: "In Jordan," she said, "if we needed to go to a shop, we would find public transportation. Here, we have to have a car, or know someone who has a car."

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.040
She began to cry as she told me she missed her family and was struggling to get used to life so far from them.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.140
Her son comforted her, and then told me, "Mom also cries whenever Messi scores."

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:53.020
Everyone in the family is taking English classes, Mufak will soon begin working as a butcher, the kids are in summer school and starting to learn to read and write in English;

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.780
in short, the situation is complicated, and hard, and hopeful - like a lot of our lives.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:05.040
But I'd argue that in every way - socially, politically, economically - we are lucky to have this family in our country.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.880
To be a refugee is to be politicized. Too often we are told that because of their ethnicity or religion, refugees are threats;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.780
that 'our' civilization is fundamentally incompatible with 'their' civilization.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:22.580
But, of course, that imagines culture as a discrete, unchanging unit, which it isn't, and it also ignores the facts:

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.500
the fact, for instance, that American soldiers in the Revolutionary War included Yusuf ben Ali and Bampett Muhamed,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:37.420
and the fact that today more than 3 million Americans are Muslims from Dave Chappelle to the owner of the Jacksonville Jaguars to my U.S. congressman, Andre Carson.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.340
Nada told me she hopes not to disappoint us...well I, in turn, hope we do not disappoint her.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:48.820
When I asked one of Nada's nephews, who's been in the U.S. for almost a year, what he liked most about his new home, he said, "the law".

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:54.320
At first the translator and I were both confused; we thought he was saying the Arabic word for 'God', but he was saying, "the order".

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:01.480
"The law", which had not existed in Syria and which had prevented him from working in Jordan, was what he liked  most about the United States.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.560
His family did not come here to change the law; they came to be protected by it.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:15.400
Hank, last week, you and HCR launched a global campaign called "With Refugees", asking people to stand with refugees by signing a petition calling on governments around the world to

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.640
1: Ensure every refugee child gets an education,
2: Ensure every refugee family has somewhere safe to live

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.120
and 3: Ensure every refugee can work or learn new skills to make a positive contribution to their community.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.080
I hope you'll join me in signing that petition. There's a link in the doobly-doo below.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:35.240
And just one last thing: that statue in Downtown Rockford of the white guy with the flag and the fireworks?

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:41.720
His name is Joe Marino. His parents immigrated to the United States from Italy in a time when many Americans believed that

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:47.820
Italian Catholics, with their loyalty to the Pope, could never be 'real' Americans participating in a democracy.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.000
And now Joe Marino is a symbol of Americanness to the people of Rockford.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:58.020
We contain multitudes, which has always strengthened us. Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

